WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.762
Dense vector retrieval is strong when the question and source use different words with similar meaning.

00:00:07.762 --> 00:00:09.647
But exact terms still matter.

00:00:09.647 --> 00:00:16.782
Think about course numbers, error codes, product names, acronyms, policy IDs, or a rare technical term.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:21.947
In those situations, keyword retrieval can outperform semantic search.

00:00:21.947 --> 00:00:23.607
BM25 is a classic sparse retrieval method.

00:00:23.607 --> 00:00:30.967
[tts-break] We tokenize the chunk text, build a BM25 index, tokenize the question, calculate scores,

00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:34.197
and return the highest-ranked chunks.

00:00:34.197 --> 00:00:39.197
This gives us a second retrieval signal that behaves differently from embeddings.

00:00:39.197 --> 00:00:41.842
The point is not to replace dense retrieval.

00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:49.722
The point is to recognize that different queries benefit from different search signals.

00:00:49.722 --> 00:00:55.531
That leads naturally to hybrid retrieval, where semantic and keyword evidence are combined.
